# STAGED AND DISABLED. The release workflow WITH crates.io publishing.
# Do not promote it until the crate exists: see ci/README.md
# ("workflows/"), which has the setup order.
#
# The file name does not end in .yml on purpose. The bulk promotion of
# 2026-09-22 copied every ci/workflows/*.yml into .github/workflows/,
# and this file went live with it while `tabnas` did not exist on
# crates.io. The live release.yml was then restored to its npm + Go
# form. A name the glob cannot match keeps that from happening again.
#
# It is a REPLACEMENT for the live .github/workflows/release.yml, not a
# new workflow: both registries' trusted-publisher entries name the file
# release.yml. To enable it, diff it against the live file, carry over
# anything the live file has gained since, and copy it over as
# .github/workflows/release.yml.
#
# Actions policy: these repos admit only GitHub-owned actions and
# Marketplace actions from verified creators, SHA-pinned.
# rust-lang/crates-io-auth-action is listed on the Marketplace under a
# verified creator (checked 2026-09-23), and the pin below is its
# v1.0.5 tag, so the policy should admit it. No run has proved that
# yet. Were it refused, the whole release would fail at startup, npm
# publish included, as rust.yml did on dtolnay/rust-toolchain.
#
# Target: tabnas/parser -> .github/workflows/release.yml
#
# Publishes @tabnas/parser to npm via GitHub OIDC Trusted Publishing (no
# token), and writes the release tags. Adapted from lsp__release.yml,
# which is where the reasoning below is worked through at length; this
# header records only what differs for parser.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. parser's release.yml was live-only —
# never staged here — which is exactly the M9 gap: ADR-16's node24 sweep
# ran over rollout/workflows/, so a workflow that was not in this
# directory was never swept. The deployed file still pins
# checkout@34e11487 # v4 and setup-node@49933ea5 # v4, both node20-
# runtime majors. Staging it fixes the pins AND puts the file under the
# applier, so the next fleet-wide change reaches it automatically.
#
# TWO TRIGGERS:
#   workflow_dispatch  the normal path. Bump in a reviewed PR first;
#                      this run reads main, publishes it, tags it. The
#                      workflow never commits.
#   push: ts/v*        the orchestrator path — admin/publish.sh pushes
#                      the tag itself, so the tag steps no-op.
#
# THE VERSION-DERIVED GENERATOR IS THE BUMP PR'S JOB, NOT THIS FILE'S.
# parser declares tabnas.release.generate ["gen-registry"], which writes
# schema/error-codes.json — and that file EMBEDS the engine version, so
# a bump alone makes it stale with no code change involved. publish.sh
# runs the generator and commits the result as part of its release
# commit. A dispatch-based release cannot do that, because it never
# commits. So the bump PR must run `npm run gen-registry` and include
# the regenerated schema in the diff. That is better, not worse: the
# regenerated file becomes something a human reviewed rather than
# something a release script produced unobserved.
#
# NO TEST STEP, for the reason the previous header already gave: some
# suites load sibling packages BY PATH (debug -> abnf), which do not
# exist in a standalone publish environment. The build DOES run, against
# already-published dependency versions, so a failing build never
# publishes. ci.yml gates the bump PR across the full matrix.
#
# Go modules are served by proxy.golang.org straight from the go/v* tag:
# PUSHING THE TAG IS THE RELEASE, and it cannot be undone, because the
# proxy caches module versions immutably. A go/vX.Y.Z naming the wrong
# commit is permanent — bump to the next patch, never move it.
#
# npm allows exactly ONE workflow file per trusted publisher, so the
# tagging has to live in this file rather than a workflow of its own; an
# OIDC token from an unregistered workflow is refused as 404 (not 403,
# so as not to leak whether a package exists). Renaming this file breaks
# publishing until the npm-side entry is updated to match.
#
# CRATES.IO IS THE SAME SHAPE, AND HAS ONE EXTRA PRECONDITION. Its
# trusted publisher config also names a single workflow FILENAME
# (owner, repo, filename, optional environment), so renaming this file
# breaks Rust publishing too, and for the same reason the crates step
# lives here rather than in a workflow of its own.
#
# The precondition is that THE CRATE MUST ALREADY EXIST. RFC 3691:
# "A Trusted Publisher Configuration can only be created after an
# initial manual publishing of a crate." So the first `tabnas` release
# goes out over a scoped token from a maintainer's machine, the trusted
# publisher is configured against the crate that publish creates, and
# every release after it comes through here. Until that is done the
# crates steps below are inert: the auth step fails and the release
# fails with it, so promote this file only once the crate exists.
#
# crates.io versions are immutable and cannot be deleted, only YANKED.
# A wrong version is therefore as permanent as a go/v tag, which is why
# the Cargo.toml version is checked against package.json before
# anything publishes.
#
# Actions are pinned to full commit SHAs with the corresponding release
# as a trailing comment, on the lowest major whose `runs.using` is
# node24 (ADR-16).

name: release

on:
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      go:
        description: 'Also tag the Go module (go/v<version>)'
        type: boolean
        default: true
  push:
    tags:
      - 'ts/v*'

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  publish:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      id-token: write   # OIDC -> npm and crates.io; cannot write refs
      contents: write   # GITHUB_TOKEN -> tags; cannot publish
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@fbc6f3992d24b796d5a048ff273f7fcc4a7b6c09  # v5.1.0
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0   # tags, for the already-released guard

      - uses: actions/setup-node@a0853c24544627f65ddf259abe73b1d18a591444  # v5.0.0
        with:
          node-version: 24
          registry-url: https://registry.npmjs.org

      # ts/package.json is the source. go/tabnas.go's const VERSION is
      # held equal to it by the fleet's version-drift tests.
      - name: Read the version
        id: v
        run: |
          V="$(node -p "require('./ts/package.json').version")"
          echo "version=$V" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          echo "Releasing $V"

      # A dispatch is NOT inherently tied to the default branch — the ref
      # is a parameter, so dispatching a version-bump feature branch would
      # publish an immutable npm version and immutable tags from an
      # unreviewed commit, bypassing the merged-main flow this header
      # promises. Refuse anything but main.
      - name: Refuse a dispatch from anywhere but main
        if: github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' && github.ref != 'refs/heads/main'
        run: |
          echo "::error::release must be dispatched from main (got ${{ github.ref }})"
          exit 1

      # Which tags this run still has to create. Fails closed only when
      # there is NOTHING left to do, which is the real "you forgot to
      # bump" signal; a run that has ts/v but is missing go/v is a
      # half-finished release and must be repairable, not refused.
      - name: Decide which tags this run must create
        id: tags
        if: github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch'
        run: |
          V="${{ steps.v.outputs.version }}"
          MISSING=""; ANCHOR=""
          for T in "ts/v$V" "go/v$V"; do
            case "$T" in
              go/*) [ "${{ inputs.go }}" = "true" ] || continue ;;
            esac
            if C="$(git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/$T^{commit}")"; then
              [ -n "$ANCHOR" ] || ANCHOR="$C"
            else
              MISSING="$MISSING $T"
            fi
          done
          MISSING="$(echo $MISSING)"
          if [ -z "$MISSING" ]; then
            echo "::error::every tag this run would create already exists — bump the version first"
            exit 1
          fi

          # A REPAIR must name the commit the release actually happened at,
          # not whatever main has become since. If ts/v landed and go/v did
          # not, and main moved on, tagging HEAD would leave the two tags
          # identifying DIFFERENT commits — permanent for the Go module,
          # because proxy.golang.org caches module versions immutably.
          HEAD_C="$(git rev-parse HEAD)"
          if [ -z "$ANCHOR" ]; then
            ANCHOR="$HEAD_C"
          elif [ "$ANCHOR" != "$HEAD_C" ]; then
            if ! npm view "@tabnas/parser@$V" version >/dev/null 2>&1; then
              echo "::error::$V is tagged at $ANCHOR but is not on npm — publishing would ship HEAD while the tags name an older commit; resolve by hand"
              exit 1
            fi
            echo "repairing an earlier release: anchoring to $ANCHOR (HEAD has moved on)"
          fi
          echo "missing=$MISSING" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          echo "anchor=$ANCHOR" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          echo "will create:$MISSING at $ANCHOR"

      # Fails closed: the schema embeds the version, so a bump PR that
      # forgot `npm run gen-registry` would publish a package whose
      # declared error-code schema names the PREVIOUS release. Cheap to
      # check here, and impossible to fix after publishing.
      - name: Refuse a stale version-derived schema
        run: |
          V="${{ steps.v.outputs.version }}"
          S="$(node -p "require('./schema/error-codes.json').version")"
          if [ "$S" != "$V" ]; then
            echo "::error::schema/error-codes.json says $S, package.json says $V — run 'npm run gen-registry' in the bump PR"
            exit 1
          fi

      # Fails closed, for the same reason the schema check does: what it
      # guards against is impossible to fix after the fact.
      # ts/package.json is the source of the version, and the fleet's
      # drift tests hold rs/Cargo.toml equal to it — but those run in
      # ci.yml on the bump PR, and this workflow deliberately has no
      # test step. Publishing 0.10.0 to npm and 0.9.7 to crates.io would
      # be permanent on both sides, so re-check it here for the price of
      # one awk. Same idiom as json's ci/rust/run.sh. It runs BEFORE the
      # npm publish: placed after it, a mismatch would stop the run with
      # npm written, crates.io not and no tags, and every re-dispatch
      # would stop at the same place.
      - name: Refuse a Rust version that disagrees with package.json
        run: |
          V="${{ steps.v.outputs.version }}"
          C="$(awk -F'"' '/^version = /{print $2; exit}' rs/Cargo.toml)"
          if [ "$C" != "$V" ]; then
            echo "::error::rs/Cargo.toml says ${C:-<missing>}, ts/package.json says $V"
            exit 1
          fi

      # Fails OPEN: the REGISTRY, not the tag, decides whether a version
      # is released. A run can publish and then fail before tagging;
      # without this the re-run dies on "cannot publish over the
      # previously published versions" before reaching the tag steps,
      # leaving a published version nothing points at.
      - name: Publish to npm if the version is missing (OIDC)
        working-directory: ts
        run: |
          V="${{ steps.v.outputs.version }}"
          npm install -g npm@latest
          if npm view "@tabnas/parser@$V" version >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            echo "@tabnas/parser@$V is already on npm — skipping publish"
          else
            npm i
            npm run build --if-present
            npm publish --access public
          fi

      # Trusted publishing: GitHub mints a short-lived OIDC identity
      # token, crates.io exchanges it for an API token good for minutes,
      # and the post step revokes it when the job ends. No secret is
      # stored anywhere. This step FAILS the run if no trusted publisher
      # is configured for the crate, which is the intended behaviour:
      # see the crates.io precondition in the header.
      - uses: rust-lang/crates-io-auth-action@c6f97d42243bad5fab37ca0427f495c86d5b1a18  # v1.0.5
        id: crates-auth

      # Fails OPEN on an already-published version, exactly as the npm
      # step does and for the same reason: a run that reached one
      # registry and died before the other must be re-dispatchable.
      # `--locked` because rs/Cargo.lock is committed and is a version
      # site in its own right; a stale lock fails here rather than being
      # silently rewritten into the published crate.
      - name: Publish to crates.io if the version is missing (OIDC)
        working-directory: rs
        env:
          CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN: ${{ steps.crates-auth.outputs.token }}
        run: |
          V="${{ steps.v.outputs.version }}"
          CODE="$(curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' \
            -A 'tabnas-release (github-actions)' \
            "https://crates.io/api/v1/crates/tabnas/$V")"
          case "$CODE" in
            200) echo "tabnas $V is already on crates.io — skipping publish" ;;
            404) cargo publish --locked ;;
            *)   echo "::error::crates.io answered $CODE for tabnas/$V; refusing to guess"
                 exit 1 ;;
          esac

      # After publishing, so a tag only ever exists for a release that
      # reached the registry.
      - name: Tag the release
        if: github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch'
        run: |
          V="${{ steps.v.outputs.version }}"
          git config user.name  'github-actions[bot]'
          git config user.email '41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com'

          # ONE atomic transaction, not a push per tag. Pushed separately,
          # ts/v could land and go/v fail on a transient error, leaving
          # npm published, ts/v tagged and the Go module unreleased — and
          # the guard above would then refuse every re-dispatch, so the
          # half-release could never be repaired. --atomic makes it
          # both-or-neither: neither is recoverable, both is done.
          for T in ${{ steps.tags.outputs.missing }}; do
            git tag "$T" "${{ steps.tags.outputs.anchor }}"
          done
          git push --atomic origin ${{ steps.tags.outputs.missing }}
